1
00:00:13,631 --> 00:00:16,115
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

2
00:00:16,135 --> 00:00:19,161
I do not say happy, but I hope it has been a happy one.

3
00:00:20,062 --> 00:00:35,286
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhudasa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhudasa

4
00:00:36,058 --> 00:00:43,856
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambuddhasa.

5
00:00:44,777 --> 00:00:48,765
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakenable.

6
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:53,136
The lights just went off and on, it's interesting.

7
00:00:54,859 --> 00:00:56,042
We'll see what happens.

8
00:00:59,548 --> 00:01:04,254
I thought today we would move on to a different discourse, so this is the one.

9
00:01:04,513 --> 00:01:08,018
We're meeting a character called Panchakanda.

10
00:01:08,037 --> 00:01:18,709
Now, Panchakanda was the carpenter to King Pasenadi, that's in the north of the Ganges, and the Buddha is at Jeta's Grove, Anathapindika's Park.

11
00:01:19,269 --> 00:01:26,096
So if you remember, Anathapindika was a rich merchant who bought him a load of land from a prince called Jeta.

12
00:01:27,037 --> 00:01:35,688
And it says that Panchakanda was his name, but I can't really believe that because it actually means five tools.

13
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:38,992
And his five tools were an adze.

14
00:01:41,213 --> 00:01:46,079
You might not know what an adze is, but I myself have seen one actually in action.

15
00:01:46,239 --> 00:01:48,341
So I'm very familiar with an adze.

16
00:01:50,403 --> 00:01:55,290
It's like a pick, a small pick, but it's only got the wide end and it's sharp.

17
00:01:55,388 --> 00:02:21,092
and it's used for shaping wood uh crudely in a way uh for instance a a trunk which you want to turn into a square beam so you would use an ads he's got a chisel which is finer he's got a measuring stick uh he's got a gavel a small hammer and he's got a blackened thread uh so that's that's that he probably walked around with those and became known as panchakanga

18
00:02:22,675 --> 00:02:23,997
Anyway, he likes discussion.

19
00:02:24,317 --> 00:02:27,401
He likes to talk about the Dharma and things.

20
00:02:27,421 --> 00:02:35,673
And he was thinking of going to see the Buddha, but he knew he'd be on alms round or something.

21
00:02:35,693 --> 00:02:44,805
So he went to see this other ascetic called Samana Mandikaputta, so the son of Mandika.

22
00:02:44,824 --> 00:02:46,707
So we'll just call him Mandika for short.

23
00:02:47,514 --> 00:02:59,497
And Mandika is in the park, in this particular park, lots of these samanas around him, these recluses, and they're all chatting away.

24
00:02:59,937 --> 00:03:05,328
And when he sees Panchakanta come, he says, sirs, be quiet, sirs, make no noise.

25
00:03:05,407 --> 00:03:10,097
Here comes the carpenter, Panchakanta, a disciple of the recluse, Gautama.

26
00:03:10,414 --> 00:03:31,527
one of the recluses uh white-clothed lay disciples staying at sovereignty that's the capital these venerable ones like quiet they are disciplined in quiet they commend quiet perhaps if he finds our assembly a quiet one he will come to join us so there's this um reputation that uh

27
00:03:31,794 --> 00:03:35,626
the Buddhist monks have that they don't make a noise when they come together.

28
00:03:36,228 --> 00:03:47,239
Now, this silence was taken to a rather far degree because lay people came to the Buddha at one point and said, look,

29
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:53,187
We approach, you know, the ascetics of other schools and teachers.

30
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:55,850
We ask them questions and they get into a discussion with us.

31
00:03:56,171 --> 00:04:02,060
But when we ask your monks and nuns questions, they just sit there like dumb pigs.

32
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:03,602
So he made a rule.

33
00:04:03,981 --> 00:04:09,590
The rule is if somebody asks me a question of the Dharma, I have to answer it.

34
00:04:10,811 --> 00:04:12,753
Or else it's an offence.

35
00:04:15,282 --> 00:04:24,312
Anyway, they've got this reputation and he gets into a conversation with Mandika.

36
00:04:25,615 --> 00:04:39,012
And Mandika tells him that, I describe somebody who's accomplished in what is wholesome, perfected in what is wholesome, attained to the supreme attainment.

37
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:43,103
an ascetic invincible with these four qualities.

38
00:04:43,543 --> 00:04:45,545
He does no evil bodily actions.

39
00:04:45,627 --> 00:04:47,528
He does no evil speech.

40
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:50,271
He has no evil intentions.

41
00:04:51,072 --> 00:04:55,216
And he does not make a living by any evil livelihood.

42
00:04:56,697 --> 00:05:01,081
So Panchakanga just listens in either approved or disapproved.

43
00:05:01,101 --> 00:05:05,927
And he wanders away thinking, well, I'll see what the blessings one thinks about that.

44
00:05:06,312 --> 00:05:09,134
So he goes to see the Blessed One and he repeats this.

45
00:05:09,896 --> 00:05:27,755
And the Blessed One replies, the Buddha replies, well, now, if this were so, carpenter, then a young, tender infant lying prone is accomplished in what is wholesome, perfected in what is wholesome, attained to the supreme attainment and ascetic invincible.

46
00:05:27,776 --> 00:05:32,380
For a young, tender infant lying prone does not even have the notion body.

47
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:35,964
So how could he do, how could he perform an evil act?

48
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:37,642
beyond mere wriggling.

49
00:05:38,603 --> 00:05:42,627
A young tender infant lying prone does not even have the notion speech.

50
00:05:43,108 --> 00:05:47,052
So how should he utter evil speech beyond mere whining?

51
00:05:47,973 --> 00:05:53,579
A young tender infant lying prone does not even have the notion intention.

52
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:59,386
So how could he have evil intentions apart from sulking?

53
00:05:59,406 --> 00:06:02,610
A young tender infant prone does not even have the notion

54
00:06:03,079 --> 00:06:27,079
livelihood so how could he make his living by evil livelihood beyond being suckled at his mother's breast so if that were so carpenter then a young tender infant lying prone is accomplished etc etc and an in an ascetic invincible so that's the uh the buddha's opinion of uh

55
00:06:27,817 --> 00:06:55,935
mandika's understanding of what it is to be uh to be fully liberated uh then having demolished that he now he now begins to tell us that uh he tells patrick anger that in fact there are 10 qualities um that i i describe as accomplished in what is wholesome as the lights again a message from the gods uh

56
00:06:57,382 --> 00:07:04,132
So the first one is, a person knows these are unwholesome habits.

57
00:07:04,572 --> 00:07:07,035
Unwholesome habits originate from this.

58
00:07:07,596 --> 00:07:10,759
Unwholesome habits cease without remainder here.

59
00:07:11,701 --> 00:07:17,749
And one practicing in this way is practicing the way to the cessation of unwholesome habits.

60
00:07:17,769 --> 00:07:20,252
So that, of course, is a sign of somebody who's liberated.

61
00:07:22,341 --> 00:07:25,725
And he also says these are wholesome habits.

62
00:07:25,764 --> 00:07:27,067
So we have wholesome habits.

63
00:07:27,367 --> 00:07:33,353
Wholesome habits originate from this and wholesome habits cease without remainder here.

64
00:07:33,572 --> 00:07:35,956
So even wholesome habits come to an end.

65
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:46,086
Now, where do unwholesome habits originate?

66
00:07:46,826 --> 00:07:49,428
They originate from the mind right now.

67
00:07:49,661 --> 00:07:52,786
This word citta is really heart-mind.

68
00:07:52,887 --> 00:08:04,444
So whenever you read mind here in Bhikkhu Bodhi's translations and in others too, remember it always includes your emotional life, your emotional commitment to things.

69
00:08:07,187 --> 00:08:15,680
So the mind is multiple, varied, and of different aspects there is a mind which is affected by greed, hatred, and delusion.

70
00:08:16,302 --> 00:08:19,146
And wholesome habits originate from this, right?

71
00:08:20,324 --> 00:08:23,088
Where do one wholesome habits cease without remainder?

72
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:33,624
Here, a person abandons bodily misconduct, develops good bodily conduct, abandons verbal misconduct and develops good verbal conduct.

73
00:08:33,923 --> 00:08:39,732
He abandons mental misconduct and develops good mental

74
00:08:40,287 --> 00:09:07,341
conduct how practicing does he practice the way to the cessation what does he do and these of course are the four right efforts the first one is he awakens zeal for the non-arising of unarisen evil states in other words uh to make sure that evil that unwholesome states don't arise and then when they do arise he arrived he awakened zeal to abandon them

75
00:09:08,231 --> 00:09:21,072
When wholesome states arise, he awakens zeal for their continuance, not their disappearance, strengthening, increase and fulfillment by developing them.

76
00:09:21,091 --> 00:09:27,422
And of course, he makes an effort and strives to bring new wholesome mental states to bear.

77
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:37,104
And it's the same with the wholesome states too.

78
00:09:37,144 --> 00:09:38,065
This is what he does, right?

79
00:09:38,086 --> 00:09:43,371
So it's always the four great efforts, whether it's wholesome or unwholesome states.

80
00:09:44,753 --> 00:09:53,604
Now, we're talking about, at the end he says, where do these wholesome habits cease without remainder?

81
00:09:53,884 --> 00:09:55,306
Their cessation is stated.

82
00:09:55,426 --> 00:10:00,312
Here a bhikkhu is virtuous, but he does not identify with his virtue.

83
00:10:00,932 --> 00:10:09,125
And he understands as he actually is the deliverance of mind and the deliverance by wisdom, where these wholesome habits cease without remainder.

84
00:10:09,485 --> 00:10:13,110
So now we come to the Arahant, somebody who's completed the path.

85
00:10:13,130 --> 00:10:15,634
And he says they are deliverance of mind.

86
00:10:16,775 --> 00:10:23,885
Now, the deliverance of mind is, of course, a purity, the deliverance from the taints, those oozings.

87
00:10:24,423 --> 00:10:30,188
And the deliverance by wisdom, of course, is the direct insight into the way things really are.

88
00:10:30,208 --> 00:10:47,668
Now, at that point, when an Arahant speaks or thinks or does something, it has this special quality of there not being a self involved, of there not being an attachment involved.

89
00:10:47,688 --> 00:10:50,312
So it's a very pure, spontaneous action.

90
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,206
You can't aim to be spontaneous.

91
00:10:54,586 --> 00:10:59,897
The whole point about spontaneity is that it arises spontaneously.

92
00:11:02,081 --> 00:11:08,993
And they have in the Abhidharma, the higher teaching, they have a special word for the actions of

93
00:11:09,208 --> 00:11:13,414
And that is kriya, which translates as sort of functional.

94
00:11:13,955 --> 00:11:21,985
So long as we are unawakened, unenlightened, our actions will always have some quality of self.

95
00:11:22,125 --> 00:11:23,828
If they're unwholesome, of course, we don't want them.

96
00:11:24,089 --> 00:11:27,693
But even when they are wholesome actions, there'll be some attachment.

97
00:11:27,953 --> 00:11:30,918
There'll be some identification with the action.

98
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:38,288
So by practicing these four great efforts, this is how we come to the end of both

99
00:11:38,673 --> 00:11:43,240
wholesome and unwholesome, wholesome and unwholesome, and wholesome actions.

100
00:11:43,679 --> 00:11:55,317
So the words in Pali are kusala, akusala, wholesome, unwholesome, and then the special category for those who are completely liberated, kriya, or functional.

101
00:12:00,504 --> 00:12:03,570
And then he goes on about wholesome intentions.

102
00:12:04,350 --> 00:12:06,333
So unwholesome intentions.

103
00:12:06,820 --> 00:12:14,071
So the first are actions and the second one is intentions, unwholesome and wholesome intentions.

104
00:12:14,952 --> 00:12:20,561
So these intentions are, of course, for sensual desire, ill will and the intention for cruelty.

105
00:12:21,462 --> 00:12:26,289
So this is the second on the Eightfold Path, if you remember, the right intention or right attitude.

106
00:12:29,174 --> 00:12:31,258
And where do these originate?

107
00:12:31,745 --> 00:12:33,427
They originate in perception.

108
00:12:33,966 --> 00:12:36,230
They originate in perception and concepts.

109
00:12:36,629 --> 00:12:39,774
So an intention is always something ahead of you.

110
00:12:39,793 --> 00:12:42,076
You're always looking into the future.

111
00:12:43,158 --> 00:12:43,758
There we go again.

112
00:12:43,778 --> 00:12:44,278
That's the lights.

113
00:12:44,318 --> 00:12:44,919
Thank you very much.

114
00:12:45,539 --> 00:12:47,422
You're always looking into the future.

115
00:12:48,182 --> 00:12:55,631
And therefore, it comes from some idea, some ideation, some ideal.

116
00:12:56,011 --> 00:13:00,537
You're always working towards some perception of where you're going to go.

117
00:13:01,275 --> 00:13:29,139
that's why an intention is based on perceptions and it's of course multiple varied of different aspects and but then but you can you can draw them down in terms of unwholesome ones to sensual desire ill will and cruelty now where do they cease well they cease by through believe it or not meditation quiet secluded from sensual pleasures secluded from unwholesome states

118
00:13:29,625 --> 00:13:33,090
A person abides in the first jhana.

119
00:13:33,409 --> 00:13:36,774
So the first absorption pushes these down.

120
00:13:36,833 --> 00:13:38,395
Remember, they don't cure them, right?

121
00:13:38,796 --> 00:13:41,578
They don't get rid of unwholesome states.

122
00:13:41,599 --> 00:13:48,167
That can only be done through allowing them to come up into consciousness and to expend their energy.

123
00:13:48,586 --> 00:13:50,909
That's part of the process of vipassana.

124
00:13:51,570 --> 00:13:54,874
But here, at least temporarily, they are suppressed.

125
00:13:56,710 --> 00:13:58,332
Now, what are wholesome intentions?

126
00:13:58,393 --> 00:13:59,955
Well, they are, of course, the opposite.

127
00:14:00,557 --> 00:14:17,384
Renunciation, instead of always grasping and acquisitiveness, non-ill will, which includes all these things about love and all that, you see, and goodwill, and the intention not to be cruel, which, of course, leads you to compassion.

128
00:14:19,035 --> 00:14:21,238
And again, they arise out of perception.

129
00:14:21,278 --> 00:14:22,840
They arise out of concepts.

130
00:14:22,860 --> 00:14:28,884
They arise out of ideals because an intention is always future bound.

131
00:14:30,947 --> 00:14:36,231
And of course, they finish without remainder.

132
00:14:36,272 --> 00:14:37,972
So where do they go without remainder?

133
00:14:38,433 --> 00:14:41,697
So that, of course, again, we're talking about the Arahant.

134
00:14:41,736 --> 00:14:43,918
We're talking about someone who's fully liberated.

135
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:48,283
And that comes about through...

136
00:14:53,173 --> 00:15:08,077
partly through the practice of jhana, but in our practice of dry vipassana, pure vipassana, it comes about through practice again.

137
00:15:10,994 --> 00:15:19,690
So again, and of course, you awaken the zeal to stop unarisen, unwholesome intentions to arise.

138
00:15:20,049 --> 00:15:23,956
And when unwholesome intentions do arise, you abandon them.

139
00:15:24,378 --> 00:15:32,192
When wholesome intentions come up, you, of course, exercise them and you try to develop even more in wholesomeness.

140
00:15:32,172 --> 00:15:49,243
So we have a final paragraph that sums up for us the ten qualities that are needed for somebody who is perfected in what is wholesome, attained to supreme attainment and is an ascetic invincible.

141
00:15:51,285 --> 00:15:53,590
So the first eight I think you'll recognize.

142
00:15:53,951 --> 00:15:56,796
Here the bhikkhu possesses right view.

143
00:15:57,264 --> 00:16:14,067
of one beyond training, the right view of an arahat, the right intention of one beyond training, the right speech of one beyond training, the right action of one beyond training, the right livelihood, the right effort, the right mindfulness and the right concentration.

144
00:16:14,847 --> 00:16:21,216
But also the number nine and number ten is right knowledge and right deliverance.

145
00:16:21,277 --> 00:16:26,023
So right knowledge, of course, is that direct perception of the deathless.

146
00:16:26,543 --> 00:16:31,879
And the right deliverance is, of course, from the taints, the oozings.

147
00:16:33,124 --> 00:16:38,881
And this is the way the Buddha describes someone who's accomplished

148
00:16:39,301 --> 00:16:47,453
in what is wholesome perfected, in what is wholesome attained in the supreme attainment, an ascetic invincible.

149
00:16:47,955 --> 00:16:56,830
That's what the Blessed One said, and the carpenter Panchakanda was satisfied and delighted in the Blessed One's words.

150
00:16:58,052 --> 00:17:04,261
So we've met another character from our discourses, and hopefully you've also found that.

151
00:17:04,613 --> 00:17:19,073
in some way inspiring, I hope, and that it will drive you relentlessly towards your liberation, which will happen hopefully sooner rather than later.

152
00:17:20,796 --> 00:17:22,458
So we can do a bit of practice.

153
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:25,362
And don't worry about the lights coming off and on.

154
00:17:25,382 --> 00:17:28,646
It's just one of those things we are suffering at the moment.

